From 816b4ab727a0b8b332631419bbb660584f528b38 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PyScotch <pyscotch@noreply.invalid>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 16:07:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/8] Bugfix: apply the Fortran null array convention to all
 arrays of `METIS_PartMeshDual()` [report R. Perry]

In `METIS_PartMeshDual()`, the Fortran convention according to which
null arrays are those equal to `ne` was applied to `options`, `tpwgts`
and `vsize`, but not to `vwgt`, which was passed as is to
`SCOTCH_graphBuild()`: the scalar `*ne` was read as a vertex weight
array, overflowing the caller's stack. Also, when the communication
volume was requested through `options[METIS_OPTION_OBJTYPE]` without
any `vsize` array, the null pointer was rebased and passed to
`_SCOTCH_METIS_OutputVol()`, which crashed for base value 1. Finally,
the return value of `SCOTCH_graphBuild()` was ignored.

The null array convention is now applied once, at the beginning of the
routine, and the local `velotab` and `vsiztab` pointers are used
throughout. A null `vsiztab` is passed as such to
`_SCOTCH_METIS_OutputVol()`, which then assumes unit sizes. The graph
is mapped only if it could be built, as `_SCOTCH_METIS_PartGraph2()`
already does.

The C and Fortran test programs now exercise the null array convention
and the computation of the communication volume without vertex sizes.

Refs: CLI-05
